              "content": "？ "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 9:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " A car travels 90 km in 150 minutes. If it continues at the same speed, what distance will it cover in 4 hours?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.24,
            0.848,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If it continues at the same speed, the ratio of the time taken should be proportional to the ratio of the distance covered."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.429,
            0.288,
            0.571,
            0.308
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "1 5 0 : 9 0 : : 4 : ?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.315,
            0.612,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is this the right way to formulate the question?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.341,
            0.848,
            0.398
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "No, because 150 is in minutes, but 4 is in hours. The second ratio should use the same units for time as the first ratio. Since 4 hours is 240 minutes, the right form is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.417,
            0.401,
            0.583,
            0.422
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_10_equation_9.jpg",
          "content": "150:90:240:?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.429,
            0.692,
            0.452
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How can you find the distance covered in 240 minutes?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.454,
            0.848,
            0.494
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Discuss with your classmates and find the answer using different strategies."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.516,
            0.837,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note to the Teacher:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Instead of giving one ‘method’ to solve the problem of the distance, encourage students to reason out the answer through different strategies. They can use their understanding of equivalent fractions and equivalent ratios to find the answer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.602,
            0.498,
            0.622
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We can model this proportion as"
